    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The first large Thracian state, formed after the Persian retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and ruled from the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tundzha">Tundzha</a>.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitalces">Sitalces</a> it reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and invaded Macedonia in 429 BCE as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</li><li>Divided after 360 BCE, it fell to Philip II and lived on as a client kingdom until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in 46 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> noted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a>, unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, made it a custom to receive gifts rather than give them, so that nothing could be done without one.</p></aside>
    </section>

