    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Odrysian Kingdom arose in the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Hebrus</a> (Maritsa) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tundzha">Tonzos</a> (Tundzha) rivers, in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and the Thracian lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> judged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> the most numerous people in the world after the Indians and thought they would be invincible if they were ever united under one ruler, but for most of their history they lived in separate tribes under their own chiefs. From the 510s BCE much of the coast and the lowlands had come under Persian control, and the Persian retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after the failed invasion of Greece in 480–479 BCE left a vacuum. Into it stepped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teres-i">Teres I</a>, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> names as the first to make their kingdom great. Teres extended his power over neighbouring tribes and made marriage alliances beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>: one of his daughters married the Scythian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariapeithes">Ariapeithes</a>. The dates of his reign are not known, and the foundation is conventionally placed around 480–460 BCE. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitalces">Sitalces</a>, who ruled from about the 440s until 424 BCE, turned this tribal hegemony into a large territorial kingdom, which Thucydides describes as reaching from Abdera on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> coast to the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rogozen-treasure"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rogozen-treasure.jpg" alt="Rogozen Treasure" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rogozen-treasure">Rogozen Treasure</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rogozen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daznaempoveche, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Odrysian society was dominated by a warrior aristocracy whose wealth is best seen in its burials. Hundreds of mound tombs dot the Thracian plain, and the richest held gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> vessels, armour, horses and imported Greek pottery. Hoards such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogozen-treasure">Rogozen</a> treasure, more than 160 silver vessels, several inscribed with the names of Odrysian kings, and the gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panagyurishte-treasure">Panagyurishte</a> treasure of the late fourth or early third century BCE show the scale of elite display. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> were farmers and herders who grew cereals and raised horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep, while the mountains yielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, silver and gold. The royal economy rested on tribute and gift-exchange. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> records that under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-i">Seuthes I</a> the yearly tribute from the tribes and the Greek cities came to about 400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, paid in gold and silver, with gifts of equal value besides, and he remarks that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a>, unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, made it a custom to receive rather than to give. Greek coastal towns such as Abdera, Maroneia, Aenus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> exchanged wine, oil and fine wares for grain, slaves, timber and metals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> wrote that the Thracians worshipped gods he equated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>, and that their kings honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> above all as their ancestor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae.jpg" alt="Battle of Thermopylae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Battle of Thermopylae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_-_Jacques-Louis_David_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Peintures_INV_3690_;_L_3711.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thracian warfare rested on light infantry and aristocratic cavalry. The peltast, armed with javelins and a light crescent-shaped wicker shield, the <em>pelte</em>, was a Thracian type that Greek armies later copied, and Thracian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> served widely in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> world. Mounted nobles fought with javelins and spears, and the Odrysian kings could also call on the warbands of subject tribes and of independent mountain peoples who joined for plunder. The greatest display of this power came in 429 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitalces">Sitalces</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, invaded Macedonia to unseat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas-ii-of-macedon">Perdiccas II</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> reports that his host was said to number about 150,000 men, a third of them cavalry; the figure is probably inflated, but the army alarmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>. Lacking the promised Athenian fleet and short of supplies, Sitalces withdrew after about a month. He was killed in 424 BCE fighting the Triballi to the north-west. Odrysian rulers also hired Greek soldiers: in 400–399 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-ii">Seuthes II</a> employed the survivors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-thousand">Ten Thousand</a> to subdue tribes near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Propontis</a>. Cotys I fought Athens for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a> in the 360s BCE, but divided Thracian forces could not withstand the reformed army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iphicrates.jpg" alt="Iphicrates" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vier_voorstellingen_uit_de_klassieke_geschiedenis_Illustraties_uit_de_klassieke_geschiedenis_(serietitel),_RP-P-1909-1633.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rijksmuseum, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Odrysian king was a paramount ruler over a federation of tribes rather than the head of a bureaucratic state. He governed through members of his own family and through subordinate princes whom Greek writers called <em>paradynasts</em>, each ruling a district and owing tribute and troops. This made the kingdom strong under capable rulers but liable to split at each succession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-i">Seuthes I</a>, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitalces">Sitalces</a>, succeeded in 424 BCE; after him power was shared between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadocus-i">Medocus</a>, often identified with Amadocus I, in the interior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-ii">Seuthes II</a>, who held lands towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Propontis</a>, and the kingdom was reunited only under Cotys I (384–360 BCE). Cotys strengthened the crown, issued coinage and married a daughter to the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a>. After his murder in 360 BCE the realm was divided between three rulers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cersobleptes">Cersobleptes</a> in the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadocus-ii">Amadocus II</a> in the centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berisades">Berisades</a> in the west, a fragmentation that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> both exploited. Odrysian rulers struck coins from the mid fifth century BCE and used Greek for inscriptions and diplomacy. A long Greek inscription from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a> records an oath involving Berenice, the queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a>, and her sons, with copies to be set up in Seuthopolis and in the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Cabyle</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak-tomb"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kazanlak-tomb.jpg" alt="Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak-tomb">Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kazanluk_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kmrakmra, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Odrysian Kingdom was the first and largest state created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, and for a century it was a power the Greek world courted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitalces">Sitalces</a> an ally in 431 BCE and granted citizenship to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadocus">Sadocus</a>, and Odrysian politics shaped Athenian strategy in the north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and the grain route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The kingdom carried Greek goods, script and coinage deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, while its metalwork combined Greek, Persian and local animal styles into a distinctive Thracian art. Its legacy is most visible today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak-tomb">Kazanlak Tomb</a>, with wall paintings of a funeral feast and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> race from around 300 BCE, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak">Kazanlak</a>, often called the Valley of the Thracian Kings, contains dozens of rich mound tombs. In one of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golyama-kosmatka">Golyama Kosmatka</a>, a bronze head found in 2004 is widely thought to portray <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a>, a fortified town of Greek plan with a royal residence, was excavated in 1948–1954 before it disappeared beneath the Koprinka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">Reservoir</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panagyurishte-treasure">Panagyurishte</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogozen-treasure">Rogozen</a> treasures have become national emblems, and modern Bulgarian identity draws heavily on the Thracian past the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a> represent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip II of Macedon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phillip_II,_king_of_Macedonia,_Roman_copy_of_Greek_original,_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek,_Copenhagen_(36420294055).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fragmentation after 360 BCE left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a> exposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>. Between 342 and 340 BCE he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cersobleptes">Cersobleptes</a>, imposed a tithe on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> and founded settlements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> (Plovdiv) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Cabyle</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took Thracian contingents to Asia, and after his death in 323 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a> fought an indecisive battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, the new Macedonian governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and later sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> against him. Seuthes ruled from his new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a> as a largely autonomous king, but Lysimachus remained overlord until his death in 281 BCE. Celtic invaders then founded the kingdom of Tylis in eastern Thrace, and for two centuries Odrysian dynasts survived as regional princes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and the Greek cities; one, Cotys, fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 171 BCE. Under Roman supremacy a Thracian client kingdom ruled by the Sapaean dynasty claimed the Odrysian inheritance. Augustus backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhoemetalces-i">Rhoemetalces I</a>, and Roman armies crushed Thracian revolts in 11 BCE and 26 CE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhoemetalces-iii">Rhoemetalces III</a> was murdered, the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> annexed the kingdom in 46 CE as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a>, ending native kingship in Thrace.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Odrysian Kingdom was the first large state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, a tribal monarchy that in the fifth and fourth centuries BCE dominated the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, dealt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> as an equal and left the gold treasures and painted tombs that define ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> today.</em></p>

